---
import { ArrowLeft, ArrowUpRight, Download } from '@lucide/astro';
import examples from '../../data/examples.json';
import { highlight } from '../../lib/highlight';
import '@fontsource-variable/geist';
import '@fontsource-variable/geist-mono';
import '../../styles/global.css';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Run the Kedi movie example with a TMDB lookup tool and typed captures."
    />
    <title>Movie night example - Kedi</title>
    <link rel="icon" href="/assets/kedi-favicon.webp" type="image/webp" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="guide-header">
      <a href="/" aria-label="kedi home" class="brand">
        <img
          class="brand-logo"
          src="/assets/kedi-logo.svg"
          width="64"
          height="44"
          alt=""
        />
        <span>kedi</span>
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href="/">
        <ArrowLeft size={16} /> Back to the homepage
      </a>
    </header>
    <main class="example-guide">
      <h1>Movie night</h1>
      <p class="guide-intro">
        Find a film by name, call TMDB through a tool, and capture its director,
        release year, and runtime as typed values.
      </p>
      <section aria-labelledby="files-title">
        <h2 id="files-title">1. Get the program</h2>
        <p>
          Download the program below. Its movie lookup tool is supplied by the
          <a href="https://registry.kedi-lang.org/package/tmdb">
            tmdb registry package
          </a>
          .
        </p>
        <div class="downloads">
          <a class="text-link" href="/examples/movie_night.kedi" download>
            <Download size={16} /> movie_night.kedi
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="setup-title">
        <h2 id="setup-title">2. Prepare the environment</h2>
        <p>
          Use Python 3.11 or newer. Until the next PyPI release, install Kedi
          from its stable branch. These commands create a separate environment
          on macOS or Linux:
        </p>
        <pre><code>uv venv --python 3.12
source .venv/bin/activate
uv pip install 'kedi @ git+https://github.com/kedi-lang/kedi@stable' 'pydantic-ai-slim[google]'
kedi add tmdb</code></pre>
        <p>
          <code>kedi add tmdb</code> installs the verified package and its
          <code>httpx</code> dependency into this environment.
        </p>
        <p>
          For other installation options, see the
          <a href="https://docs.kedi-lang.org/getting-started/installation/">
            installation guide
          </a>
          .
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="https://registry.kedi-lang.org/package/tmdb">Install tmdb</a>
          from the registry with <code>kedi add tmdb</code>.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="keys-title">
        <h2 id="keys-title">3. Configure your keys</h2>
        <p>
          In that directory, create a local <code>.env</code> with your Google
          API key and your TMDB <strong>API Read Access Token</strong>. The TMDB
          token is sent as a Bearer header; it is not the shorter API key.
        </p>
        <pre><code>GOOGLE_API_KEY=your-google-api-key
TMDB_API_KEY=your-tmdb-read-access-token</code></pre>
        <p>
          Keep <code>.env</code> out of version control. The keys are used by
          your local program, never by this website.
          <a href="https://developer.themoviedb.org/docs/getting-started">
            TMDB setup <ArrowUpRight size={13} />
          </a>
        </p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="run-title">
        <h2 id="run-title">4. Run the program</h2>
        <pre><code>kedi movie_night.kedi --adapter-model google/gemini-3-flash-preview</code></pre>
        <p>
          The model can call <code>movie_details</code> to search for the title
          and read its details. The template binds three values; the final
          statement returns the director. Model and TMDB requests use your
          accounts.
        </p>
        <div class="code-body">
          <pre><code>{examples.hello.code.split('\n').map(line => <span class="guide-code-line">{highlight(line).map(token => <span class={token.kind ? `syntax-${token.kind}` : undefined}>{token.text}</span>)}{'\n'}</span>)}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p>
          For a remake or an ambiguous title, include its release year in the
          template. The tool accepts a year and rejects ambiguous matches.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  .guide-header,
  .example-guide {
    width: min(820px, calc(100% - 40px));
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
  .guide-header {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 20px;
    padding-block: 24px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .example-guide {
    padding-block: 48px 80px;
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: 42px;
  }
  .guide-intro {
    margin-top: 18px;
    font-size: 18px;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  section {
    margin-top: 38px;
  }
  h2 {
    font-size: 22px;
    text-wrap: initial;
  }
  p {
    margin-top: 16px;
  }
  p a {
    color: var(--green-dark);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
  p svg {
    display: inline;
    vertical-align: middle;
  }
  pre {
    margin-top: 18px;
    padding: 18px;
    background: var(--white);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .downloads {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 12px 28px;
    margin-top: 18px;
  }
  .code-body {
    margin-top: 20px;
  }
  .code-body pre {
    margin: 0;
    border: 0;
  }
  .guide-code-line {
    white-space: pre-wrap;
  }
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    h1 {
      font-size: 34px;
    }
    .example-guide {
      padding-top: 32px;
    }
  }
</style>
